WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.355
Vision transformers are powerful but data-hungry:
they only beat convolutional networks

00:00:06.435 --> 00:00:09.152
when huge labeled datasets are available.

00:00:09.232 --> 00:00:11.267
This paper introduces PatchRot,

00:00:11.347 --> 00:00:16.247
a self-supervised technique crafted specifically
for vision transformers.

00:00:16.327 --> 00:00:17.816
The key idea is simple.

00:00:17.896 --> 00:00:23.274
Rotate the whole image or its individual patches by
multiples of ninety degrees,

00:00:23.354 --> 00:00:27.231
and train the transformer to predict every rotation angle.

00:00:27.311 --> 00:00:32.143
The class token predicts the image rotation,
capturing global structure,

00:00:32.223 --> 00:00:37.600
while new per-patch heads predict each patch's
rotation, capturing local detail.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.169
After this pretraining,

00:00:39.249 --> 00:00:46.947
PatchRot features consistently beat both supervised
training from scratch and the RotNet baseline across CIFAR-10,

00:00:47.027 --> 00:00:49.880
CIFAR-100, FashionMNIST, and Tiny-ImageNet.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:55.250
Vision transformers have overtaken convolutional
networks on many vision tasks,

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:58.652
but only when trained on very large labeled datasets.

00:00:58.732 --> 00:01:02.503
With limited labels their performance falls behind
ConvNets,

00:01:02.583 --> 00:01:08.087
because they lack built-in inductive biases like
locality and translation equivariance.

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:11.873
Labeling data at the scale ViTs need is expensive
and slow.

00:01:11.953 --> 00:01:16.815
Self-supervised learning can help by learning
useful features without labels,

00:01:16.895 --> 00:01:22.591
but the popular self-supervised pretext tasks were
all designed for convolutional networks

00:01:22.671 --> 00:01:27.020
and ignore the patch-token structure that makes
transformers special.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.060
A vision transformer splits an image into patches
and applies self-attention,

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:37.170
so unlike a convolutional network it can produce a
separate output for every patch,

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.632
not just one output for the whole image.

00:01:39.712 --> 00:01:44.865
Prior work called RotNet showed that simply
predicting the rotation angle of an image

00:01:44.945 --> 00:01:48.497
teaches a convolutional network surprisingly rich
features.

00:01:48.577 --> 00:01:53.483
The natural question is: can we push rotation
prediction down to the patch level,

00:01:53.563 --> 00:01:59.885
so the transformer learns local features for each
patch as well as global structure for the whole image?

00:01:59.965 --> 00:02:04.810
That patch-level signal is exactly what a
token-based model is built to exploit.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:08.836
The paper makes three main contributions.

00:02:08.916 --> 00:02:12.562
First, it introduces PatchRot,

00:02:12.642 --> 00:02:16.287
a self-supervised technique crafted for vision
transformers that predicts rotation angles

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:20.013
at two levels: the class token predicts the
whole-image rotation for global context,

00:02:20.093 --> 00:02:23.739
and new per-patch heads predict each patch's
rotation for local detail.

00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:27.465
Second,

00:02:27.545 --> 00:02:31.191
it introduces a buffer gap between patches during
training so the network cannot cheat by matching continuous edges,

00:02:31.271 --> 00:02:34.917
forcing it to learn genuine content.

00:02:34.997 --> 00:02:38.642
Third,

00:02:38.722 --> 00:02:42.368
it demonstrates through extensive experiments that
PatchRot beats both supervised training

00:02:42.448 --> 00:02:46.094
from scratch and the RotNet baseline across
multiple datasets,

00:02:46.174 --> 00:02:49.820
and that its features transfer well and help in
semi-supervised settings.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.767
PatchRot works as follows.

00:02:51.847 --> 00:02:55.189
Take an input image and either rotate the whole image,

00:02:55.269 --> 00:02:57.977
or rotate each of its patches independently,

00:02:58.057 --> 00:03:03.109
by a random multiple of ninety degrees: zero,
ninety, one-eighty, or two-seventy.

00:03:03.189 --> 00:03:09.953
The vision transformer is then trained to predict
these rotation angles as a simple four-way classification.

00:03:10.033 --> 00:03:14.705
The transformer's class token, which normally
predicts the object category,

00:03:14.785 --> 00:03:19.647
is repurposed to predict the whole-image rotation,
capturing global structure.

00:03:19.727 --> 00:03:26.554
New multilayer-perceptron heads are attached to
each patch token to predict that individual patch's rotation,

00:03:26.634 --> 00:03:28.011
capturing local detail.

00:03:28.091 --> 00:03:30.355
To prevent the network from cheating,

00:03:30.435 --> 00:03:34.664
patches are carved from a slightly larger grid and
randomly cropped,

00:03:34.744 --> 00:03:40.240
so a random buffer gap sits between them and edge
continuity can't give away the answer.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.838
Training happens at a reduced resolution;

00:03:42.918 --> 00:03:48.350
then the extra patch heads are removed and the
network is fine-tuned at full resolution

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:50.378
on the real classification task,

00:03:50.458 --> 00:03:54.560
with positional embeddings interpolated to the
larger patch count.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:04:00.539
The authors test PatchRot on four standard image
classification datasets: CIFAR-10,

00:04:00.619 --> 00:04:04.850
CIFAR-100, FashionMNIST at thirty-two by thirty-two
resolution,

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.998
and Tiny-ImageNet at sixty-four by sixty-four.

00:04:08.078 --> 00:04:13.404
They also study SVHN and MNIST to probe
rotation-invariant objects like digits.

00:04:13.484 --> 00:04:18.125
The backbone is a compact vision transformer with
six encoder blocks.

00:04:18.205 --> 00:04:23.531
Patch sizes are four pixels for the small datasets
and eight for Tiny-ImageNet,

00:04:23.611 --> 00:04:27.158
with a buffer gap set to a quarter of the patch size.

00:04:27.238 --> 00:04:29.074
Beyond plain classification,

00:04:29.154 --> 00:04:33.384
they evaluate transfer learning between CIFAR-10
and CIFAR-100,

00:04:33.464 --> 00:04:38.380
and a semi-supervised setting on CIFAR-10 where
only a handful of labels,

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:42.280
from two hundred fifty up to ten thousand, are available.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:48.348
The headline result is that PatchRot pretraining
consistently outperforms both supervised

00:04:48.428 --> 00:04:51.913
training from scratch and the RotNet rotation baseline,

00:04:51.993 --> 00:04:53.468
on every dataset tested.

00:04:53.548 --> 00:04:55.672
On CIFAR-10 with full fine-tuning,

00:04:55.752 --> 00:04:59.755
PatchRot reaches ninety-two point six percent
top-one accuracy,

00:04:59.835 --> 00:05:05.005
compared to eighty-three point nine percent for
supervised training from scratch.

00:05:05.085 --> 00:05:12.329
On CIFAR-100 the gap is even larger: seventy point
six percent top-one versus fifty point two percent supervised,

00:05:12.409 --> 00:05:14.792
and ninety point two percent top-five.

00:05:14.872 --> 00:05:20.041
On FashionMNIST it improves ninety-four point one
versus eighty-nine point eight.

00:05:20.121 --> 00:05:22.115
Remarkably, even linear probing,

00:05:22.195 --> 00:05:26.717
where the entire network is frozen and only the
final layer is trained,

00:05:26.797 --> 00:05:29.115
gets close to supervised performance,

00:05:29.195 --> 00:05:34.300
and fine-tuning just a single encoder block already
beats training from scratch.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:39.885
The ablation study on CIFAR-10 confirms that every
component of PatchRot matters.

00:05:39.965 --> 00:05:44.060
Training on patch rotations alone, without
whole-image rotation,

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:49.801
drops accuracy to ninety-one point eight percent
because the model loses global context.

00:05:49.881 --> 00:05:55.282
Using only image rotation, essentially the RotNet
approach adapted to a transformer,

00:05:55.362 --> 00:05:57.500
drops it to ninety-one point zero.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:01.479
Rotating the image and its patches together in a
single pass,

00:06:01.559 --> 00:06:04.742
rather than in separate passes, hurts performance,

00:06:04.822 --> 00:06:09.830
as does training at the original resolution instead
of the reduced resolution,

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:14.397
and reusing the existing head instead of adding
dedicated patch heads.

00:06:14.477 --> 00:06:19.160
Each of these lands below the full method's
ninety-two point six percent,

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.900
showing every design decision contributes.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:24.237
The numbers tell a clear story.

00:06:24.317 --> 00:06:29.232
On CIFAR-10, PatchRot reaches ninety-two point six
percent top-one accuracy,

00:06:29.312 --> 00:06:34.555
an improvement of eight point seven points over
supervised training from scratch.

00:06:34.635 --> 00:06:37.907
On CIFAR-100 the improvement is over twenty points,

00:06:37.987 --> 00:06:41.718
from fifty point two to seventy point six percent top-one,

00:06:41.798 --> 00:06:44.216
and ninety point two percent top-five.

00:06:44.296 --> 00:06:50.064
FashionMNIST improves to ninety-four point one
percent and Tiny-ImageNet's top-five rises

00:06:50.144 --> 00:06:52.430
to seventy-three point four percent.

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:57.622
In the semi-supervised setting with only four
thousand labeled CIFAR-10 images,

00:06:57.702 --> 00:07:00.579
PatchRot reaches eighty-one percent accuracy,

00:07:00.659 --> 00:07:05.640
versus roughly fifty-four percent for supervised
training on the same labels,

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:10.700
showing how valuable the self-supervised features
are when labels are scarce.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:13.489
The takeaway is that a very simple idea,

00:07:13.569 --> 00:07:18.094
predicting the rotation of both the whole image and
each individual patch,

00:07:18.174 --> 00:07:23.010
turns out to be a self-supervised task perfectly
suited to vision transformers.

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:28.423
Because the class token learns global structure and
the patch heads learn local detail,

00:07:28.503 --> 00:07:32.530
PatchRot teaches the transformer rich features
without any labels,

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:39.437
and those features reliably beat supervised
training from scratch and the RotNet baseline across four datasets,

00:07:39.517 --> 00:07:43.482
in transfer learning, and especially when labeled
data is scarce.

00:07:43.562 --> 00:07:44.726
It is a lightweight,

00:07:44.806 --> 00:07:49.020
practical recipe for pretraining vision
transformers on limited data.
